WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Speaker 1: I was actually surprised how many of&nbsp;
you wrote to me about this story in the few hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.320
before we started producing today's show, and I&nbsp;
want to talk about it because what's at issue is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:18.480
something actually kind of subtle. The headlines&nbsp;
are Trump accuses the January 6th Committee of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.720
seeking evidence of criminal activity, and a lot&nbsp;
of you wrote to me and said, How is how is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.840
an accusation? That's obviously the point. That's&nbsp;
what the Jan. six panel is supposed to be doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.160
But what's that at issue here is that it is not&nbsp;
actually the job of Congress to seek information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:44.960
about crimes if they find information about crimes&nbsp;
in doing their investigation. They should and can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.040
turn it over to the Department of Justice. But&nbsp;
Trump's allegation is that that is actually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.000
prime motive for what they are doing. So there's&nbsp;
an Axios article that that sort of explains this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.320
Former Trump's legal former President Trump's&nbsp;
legal team accused the House Committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.800
investigating the Trump riots of seeking&nbsp;
to uncover evidence that would support a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.560
criminal referral against him. Why it matters&nbsp;
The brief asked the Supreme Court to consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.840
Committee Chair Bennie Thompson's interview with&nbsp;
The Washington Post as part of its effort to block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:20.000
the release of records to the committee last week.&nbsp;
Trump's lawyers asked them to block the release of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.560
documents and records from his administration,&nbsp;
arguing that their release would undermine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.360
certain presidential privileges. In an interview&nbsp;
published the same day, Thompson said the select&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:36.080
committee was examining Trump's actions on Jan.&nbsp;
six as it considers to recommend that the Justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:42.000
Department open a criminal investigation into&nbsp;
Trump. The select committee So Trump is arguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.040
the select committee is acting as an inquisitorial&nbsp;
tribunal seeking evidence of criminal activity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:55.360
and that doing so is quote outside of any of&nbsp;
Congress's legislative powers. The Washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:01.760
Post has confirmed what was already apparent. The&nbsp;
committee is indeed seeking any excuse to refer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.480
a political rival for criminal charges, and&nbsp;
they are using the using this investigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:13.680
to do so. We're expecting a response from the&nbsp;
committee today. So again, if your reaction is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.080
of course, that's what they're doing, that's&nbsp;
the point. It's actually not the point of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:24.160
Congress. The job of Congress is not to seek as&nbsp;
a primary objective information about crimes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.320
What we would imagine I spoke to one lawyer&nbsp;
this morning about it. What we would expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:38.480
the committee to say is we are investigating&nbsp;
what happened, who caused it. How could it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:44.160
have been prevented? How can we make sure that&nbsp;
it doesn't happen again if in so investigating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.360
those questions, we come across evidence of&nbsp;
criminality by Donald Trump or anybody else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.440
in our opinion, because remember, they are&nbsp;
not law enforcement. If, in their opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.520
there is evidence of criminality or at least&nbsp;
potential evidence to suggest an investigation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.080
they would go to the Department of Justice and&nbsp;
say, You might want to take a look at this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.480
and the DOJ would then make a decision as to&nbsp;
whether to open a formal investigation or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.880
So there is some subtlety there, and obviously&nbsp;
there is room for interpretation and sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:19.440
squishiness there. Meanwhile, the claim from Trump&nbsp;
is their only purpose. Their only goal is to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.960
crimes I may have committed or that they believe&nbsp;
that I committed. And that is not actually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:30.000
duty of a congressional committee and that that&nbsp;
is that is accurate. That would actually be true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:38.080
I think at the end of the day, it's&nbsp;
going to be very hard to make the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:46.240
You sort of have to go to intent. And if you&nbsp;
want to prevent access or try to shut down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.960
the committee or whatever else the case may&nbsp;
be, you really have to get into the intent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.240
in someone's mind. Sort of like with perjury, did&nbsp;
you unknowingly lie or did you deliberately lie?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.240
And it's going to be very difficult from what&nbsp;
I'm reading to successfully argue that the sole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.720
purpose or the primary purpose or&nbsp;
the true purpose of the committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.960
is to find evidence of Trump's criminality. And I&nbsp;
think if you look at the genesis of the committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:18.880
in and of itself, it's pretty clear that while it&nbsp;
may be a bonus, it may be a tertiary or subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.480
priority to know whether Donald Trump committed&nbsp;
crimes. You're not going to be able to prove that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:32.960
that's really the point of the investigation. It's&nbsp;
also relevant to say that in an abstract sense, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:40.640
is not the Legislature's function to investigate&nbsp;
crimes, but the Legislature has a clear function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:48.080
in creating law and to create law. You need to&nbsp;
understand what took place and how what took place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:55.120
relates to the existing law that's completely&nbsp;
legitimate. You need to determine does current law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:02.240
cover what took place or not? This was an issue&nbsp;
with the. Remember this whole child porn case in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.760
New York State, the details I don't remember,&nbsp;
but there was some technicality in how the law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:15.440
used to be written, where viewing child porn and&nbsp;
possessing it were two different things based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:22.000
browser cache and some technical elements.&nbsp;
And after someone was acquitted or charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.720
were dropped on that technicality, I think&nbsp;
it was, the New York Legislature said. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:33.040
got to rewrite how we define possession of child&nbsp;
porn. We're talking about something similar here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:40.560
and the House Committee can very legitimately,&nbsp;
from what I'm reading, say we have to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:47.200
what took place in relation to the law because we&nbsp;
are charged with writing law. And if in so doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.640
we come across the belief that laws were broken&nbsp;
by Trump or anyone else. We refer it to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.840
Department of Justice. I think they are. They are&nbsp;
ultimately going to have that argument prevail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.000
And again, strictly speaking, strictly&nbsp;
speaking, if you want to defend yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.880
on process rather than on substance, which is&nbsp;
what Donald Trump is doing that is completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.480
legitimate in our legal system. It is completely&nbsp;
legitimate to get yourself acquitted because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:20.640
the people investigating you did not use the&nbsp;
right processes. It's completely legitimate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:26.160
colloquially speaking. Donald Trump's continued&nbsp;
behavior is not the behavior of an innocent man.

